/ref 17592186493663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493876 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493876 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493876 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493876 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493876 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cc6a12d1-cfbc-5af0-9418-2c899316c53c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493878 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493878 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493878 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493878 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493878 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493878 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493878 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493878 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493878 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "df699b32-bb28-5240-a2c7-ff70f867d7f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493898 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493898 #edb/ref 1069 "q-325131" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493898 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493898 #edb/ref 1071 "The weekly exercise time for a community is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ minutes and a standard deviation of $30$ minutes. Additionally, $60%$ of people exercise between $0.9\\mu $ minutes and $1.1\\mu $ minutes each week. Find the percentage of people who exercise less than $220$ minutes per week. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1020\\,$ | $\\,0.1003\\,$ | $\\,0.0985\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1210\\,$ | $\\,0.1190\\,$ | $\\,0.1170\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1423\\,$ | $\\,0.1401\\,$ | $\\,0.1379\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the people exercise less than $220$ minutes per week." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493898 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b9f0001-1223-5b0c-bd32-5cd32bc54dbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493898 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the weekly exercise time of a randomly selected person. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{30}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<220).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu )=0.60.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.30 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.30 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.80 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{30}) & =0.80 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{30}) & =0.80 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{30} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.80).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.80)=0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{30} & =0.8416 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =25.248 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{25.248}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈252.48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<220)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<220) & =P(Z<\\frac{220-252.48}{30}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.08) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.08) \\\\  & =0.1401 \\\\  & ≈14.01%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $14.01%$ of the people exercise less than $220$ minutes per week." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493899 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493899 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95073" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493899 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493899 #edb/ref 1071 "A group of students took a test and got an average score of $81.$ Additionally, $5%$ of the students scored greater than $91.$ Assuming that the students' scores are normally distributed, find the percent of students with a score lower than $70,$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0287\\,$ | $\\,0.0281\\,$ | $\\,0.0274\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0359\\,$ | $\\,0.0351\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0446\\,$ | $\\,0.0436\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493899 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e826c15-fd0c-50c0-b8b3-b16460ce62c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493899 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the grade obtained by a student. Then we have $X∼N(81,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<70).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>91)=0.05.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 91) & =1-P(X>91) \\\\  & =1-0.05 \\\\  & =0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 91) & =0.95 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{91-81}{\\sigma }) & =0.95 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{10}{\\sigma }) & =0.95 \\\\ \\frac{10}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.95)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.95)≈1.6449.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{\\sigma } & =1.6449 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.16449 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.16449} \\\\  & ≈6.0794.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<70)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<70) & =P(X\\le 70) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{70-81}{6.0794}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.81) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.81) \\\\  & =0.0351 \\\\  & ≈3.5%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $3.5%$ of the students scored lower than $70.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493902 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493902 #edb/ref 1069 "q-324300" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493902 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493902 #edb/ref 1071 "Scores on a certain standardized exam are normally distributed with a mean of $75$ and a standard deviation of $8.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected student scores less than $68?$ **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ | $\\,0.1635\\,$ | $\\,0.1611\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ | $\\,0.1894\\,$ | $\\,0.1867\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2206\\,$ | $\\,0.2177\\,$ | $\\,0.2148\\,$ |\n\nThe probability that a randomly selected student scores less than $68$ is approximately\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493902 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "29065405-ccc0-5f24-b571-a168ed04c45b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493902 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the exam score. Then we have $X∼N(75,{8}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X<68).$ First, we write this as a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\nP(X<68)=P(X\\le 68)\n$$\n\nThen, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 68) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{68-75}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -0.88) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.88)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.88)=0.1894≈0.19.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected student scores less than $68$ is approximately $0.19.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493917 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493917 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133055" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493917 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493917 #edb/ref 1071 "The output voltage of a particular model of laptop AC adapters is $19$ volts on average. However, $1%$ of adapters have a voltage greater than $20$ volts. Assuming that the output voltage is normally distributed, find the percent of AC adapters with a voltage greater than $19.3$ volts, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493917 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4286abc9-bbfc-5418-a3f8-c44338b9b405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493917 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the output voltage of a randomly selected AC adapter. Then we have $X∼N(19,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>19.3).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>20)=0.01.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =1-P(X>20) \\\\  & =1-0.01 \\\\  & =0.99\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =0.99 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{20-19}{\\sigma }) & =0.99 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{1}{\\sigma }) & =0.99 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.99)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.99)≈2.3263.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =2.3263 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{2.32639} \\\\  & ≈0.42985.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X>19.3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>19.3) & =1-P(X\\le 19.3) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le \\frac{19.3-19}{0.42985}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z\\le 0.70) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.70) \\\\  & =1-0.7580 \\\\  & ≈24.2%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $24.2%$ of AC adapters have a voltage greater than $19.3$ volts." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493926 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493926 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493926 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493926 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following data sets **might** be normally distributed?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493926 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53a86081-094e-5bab-a3ac-f318e3e80fd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493926 #edb/ref 1074 "If a large data set is normally distributed, it should be approximately bell-shaped. \n\nAmong the given options, the only bell-shaped curve is the following:\n\n![](images/00/0051f030962cd0a47503349f993ae1bb8faf90f47434213f9106fd30a69f6c60.png)\n\nIt's important to note that other bell-shaped distributions exist. Therefore, further tests on the data are required to confirm whether the underlying distribution is normal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493933 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493933 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94978" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493933 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493933 #edb/ref 1071 "The diastolic blood pressure of a group of people is normally distributed with a mean of $85\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and a standard deviation of $14\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected person from the group has a diastolic blood pressure greater than $77\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and less than $86\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}?$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2546\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2514\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2483\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2877\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2843\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2810\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3228\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3192\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3156\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4364\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4325\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4286\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5239\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5279\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5319\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5636\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5675\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5714\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493933 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "865ccf64-fc88-5f41-a8ec-576978864deb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493933 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the diastolic blood pressure of the selected person. Then we have $X∼N(85,{14}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(77<X<86).$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(77<X<86) & =P(\\frac{77-85}{14}<Z<\\frac{86-85}{14}) \\\\  & =P(-0.57<Z<0.07)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.57<Z<0.07) & =P(Z<0.07)-P(Z\\le -0.57) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.07)-P(Z\\le -0.57) \\\\  & =Φ(0.07)-Φ(-0.57)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.07) & =0.5279 \\\\ Φ(-0.57) & =0.2843\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.57<Z<0.07) & =Φ(0.07)-Φ(-0.57) \\\\  & =0.5279-0.2843 \\\\  & =0.2436.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the randomly selected person from the group has a diastolic blood pressure greater than $77\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and less than $86\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ is approximately $0.24.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493938 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493938 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139249" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493938 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493938 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following data sets **might** be normally distributed?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493938 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9a6a1966-1356-568d-b897-5f5f7b21ff10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493938 #edb/ref 1074 "If a large data set is normally distributed, it should be approximately bell-shaped. \n\nAmong the given options, the only bell-shaped curve is the following:\n\n![](images/34/342a545437fc0884e69bb31d383a6e121c9cb0a1db08eab4d702cf9a0b296075.png)\n\nIt's important to note that other bell-shaped distributions exist. Therefore, further tests on the data are required to confirm whether the underlying distribution is normal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493946 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139242" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493946 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493946 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following data sets **might** be normally distributed?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493946 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac482591-94cf-5da7-a1f4-becc288b5be6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493946 #edb/ref 1074 "If a large data set is normally distributed, it should be approximately bell-shaped. \n\nAmong the given options, the only bell-shaped curve is the following:\n\n![](images/ff/ffbfb11f7cb0cef6cee8ccbb44aace9df2fdc0f831f5d78ad92a8f696b6ccffc.png)\n\nIt's important to note that other bell-shaped distributions exist. Therefore, further tests on the data are required to confirm whether the underlying distribution is normal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493962 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493962 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95126" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493962 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493962 #edb/ref 1071 "The lengths of babies at birth in a given population are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{cm}$ and a standard deviation of $6\\,\\text{cm}.$ Additionally, the length of $60%$ of the newborns falls between $0.9\\mu \\,\\text{cm}$ and $1.1\\mu \\,\\text{cm}.$ Find the mean $\\mu $ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493962 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d916a01a-c116-520b-be92-bf93eddd6997" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493962 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the length of a randomly selected newborn. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{6}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu )=0.6.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.6 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.6 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.3 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.3 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.8 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{6}) & =0.8 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{6}) & =0.8 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{6} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.8)≈0.8416.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{6} & =0.8416 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =5.0496 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{5.0496}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈50.50\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493970 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493970 #edb/ref 1069 "q-324457" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493970 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493970 #edb/ref 1071 "In a distribution center, the average weight of shipped packages is $2.5$ kilograms. However, $20%$ of packages weigh no more than $2.3$ kilograms. Assuming that package weights are normally distributed, find the standard deviation, $\\sigma ,$ of the package weights, **rounded to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$kg$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493970 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e22ccace-d75b-5bc7-acc2-7090937bcda1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493970 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the package weight (in kilograms). Then we have $X∼N(2.5,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 2.3)=0.20.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2.3) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2.3-2.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{0.2}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ -\\frac{0.2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.20)=-0.8416.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{0.2}{\\sigma } & =-0.8416 \\\\ \\frac{0.2}{\\sigma } & =0.8416 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.2}{0.8416} \\\\  & ≈0.24\\,\\text{kg}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493971 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493971 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132434" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493971 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493971 #edb/ref 1071 "The volume of soda in cans filled at a particular factory is normally distributed with a mean of $12$ ounces and a standard deviation of $0.1$ ounces. What is the probability that a randomly selected soda can contains more than $12.2$ ounces? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9772\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9821\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9861\\,$ | $\\,0.9864\\,$ | $\\,0.9868\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493971 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e312f711-a596-58e6-bba4-4f3bc71c5f9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493971 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the volume (in ounces) of soda in a randomly selected can. Then we have $X∼N(12,{0.1}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X>12.2).$ Thus, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>12.2) & =P(Z>\\frac{12.2-12}{0.1}) \\\\  & =P(Z>2) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-Φ(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n1-Φ(2)=1-0.9772≈0.02.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected soda can contains more than $12.2$ ounces is approximately $0.02.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493975 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493975 #edb/ref 1069 "q-325123" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493975 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493975 #edb/ref 1071 "The average amount of coffee dispensed into medium cups is $10$ ounces. Additionally, in $15%$ of cups, the coffee amount is less than $9.1$ ounces. Assuming the amount dispensed is normally distributed, find the percentage of cups containing less than $8.5$ ounces of coffee. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $\\,p\\,$ | $0.01$ | $0.03$ | $0.05$ | $0.10$ | $0.15$ | $0.20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z).$*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ | $\\,0.0336\\,$ | $\\,0.0329\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ | $\\,0.0418\\,$ | $\\,0.0409\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0526\\,$ | $\\,0.0516\\,$ | $\\,0.0505\\,$ |\n\nApprximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of cups contain less than $8.5$ ounces of coffee." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493975 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fe644563-13d6-5255-adb7-59462252c8b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493975 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the amount of coffee in a randomly selected cup. Then we have $X∼N(10,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<8.5).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nWe know that $P(X<9.1)=0.15.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<9.1) & =0.15 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{9.1-10}{\\sigma }) & =0.15 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma }) & =0.15 \\\\ \\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.15)=-1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma } & =-1.0364 \\\\ \\frac{0.9}{\\sigma } & =1.0364 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.9}{1.0364} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈0.8684.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<8.5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<8.5) & =P(X\\le 8.5) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{8.5-10}{0.8684}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.73) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.73) \\\\  & =0.0418 \\\\  & ≈4.18%.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $4.18%$ of coffee cups contain less than $8.5$ ounces of coffee." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
